## 4. Technical Findings

### 4.1 Phase 1 — Reconnaissance (Nmap)

**Command used:**
```
sudo nmap -sV -O 172.17.0.0/24 -oN nmap_results.txt
```

**Date/time:** 2026-08-13 21:05 -0400
**Target range:** 172.17.0.0/24 (Docker bridge network hosting the test environment)

**Scan summary:**

| Host (IP) | Role | OS Guess | Open Ports | Services & Versions |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 172.17.0.2 | DVWA web application container | Linux (no exact match — Docker networking limits OS fingerprint accuracy) | 80/tcp | Apache httpd 2.4.25 (Debian) |
| 172.17.0.1 | Docker bridge gateway (Kali host) | Linux 5.0–6.2 (96% confidence, aggressive guess) | 22/tcp | OpenSSH 10.3p1 Debian 4 (protocol 2.0) |

**Observations:**
- **172.17.0.2 (DVWA):** Apache httpd 2.4.25 is a 2016-era release. While DVWA is intentionally configured as a vulnerable training target, in a production context this version would warrant verification of the underlying Debian security-patch level, since Debian backports fixes without always bumping the displayed version string. Only one service (HTTP) is exposed on this host — a reasonably minimal attack surface for what is otherwise designed to be maximally vulnerable at the application layer.
- **172.17.0.1 (Docker gateway):** SSH is reachable from the container bridge network. This is expected in a Docker/Kali lab setup and low-risk in isolation, but represents a management interface exposed to the same network segment as a deliberately vulnerable web app — worth noting as a defense-in-depth observation (network segmentation) rather than a direct vulnerability.
- OS fingerprinting returned no exact matches for either host — normal behavior for Nmap against Docker's virtualized networking, and noted here rather than treated as a scan failure.

