## 6. Remediation Roadmap

| Priority | Finding ID | Fix | Effort | Owner | Target Timeline |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | F-003 | Enforce HTTPS/TLS site-wide; redirect HTTP→HTTPS; enable HSTS | Medium | Web/App team | Immediate (0–3 days) |
| 2 | F-004 | Disable directory indexing on `/config/`; move sensitive config outside web root | Easy | Sysadmin | 3–7 days |
| 3 | F-001 | Upgrade Apache to a currently supported release / verify patch level | Medium | Sysadmin | 7 days |
| 4 | F-006 | Add `HttpOnly` (and `Secure`, post-TLS) flags to session cookies | Easy | Web/App team | 7 days |
| 5 | F-005 | Disable directory indexing on `/docs/` | Easy | Sysadmin | 14 days |
| 6 | F-002 | Restrict SSH exposure on the container bridge network; enforce key-based auth | Easy | Sysadmin | 14 days |
| 7 | F-007 | Add missing security headers (HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, CSP, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy) | Easy | Web/App team | 30 days |
| 8 | F-008 | Remove `.gitignore` and other build artifacts from the public web root | Easy | Sysadmin | 30 days |

**Prioritization logic:** The single Critical finding (F-003) is addressed first regardless of effort, since it directly exposes user credentials. The two High findings (F-004, and the associated config-exposure risk) follow immediately after, both being low-effort fixes with outsized risk reduction. Remaining Medium and Low findings are sequenced by effort — easy wins first — since none individually block the higher-priority items.

